4,Lamkang language,https://grokipedia.com/page/lamkang_language,"Lamkang belongs to the Sino-Tibetan language family, specifically the Tibeto-Burman branch, and is classified within the Kuki-Chin subgroup as a Northern Kuki-Chin language, also known as part of the ""Old Kuki"" languages.[1]...",Lamkang,Sino-Tibetan
5,All In The Language Family: The Sino-Tibetan Languages,https://www.babbel.com/en/magazine/sino-tibetan-language-family,"The Sino-Tibetan family has 400 languages. Fortunately for us, we can break this language family into 4 slightly more bite-sized branches. First up is Sinitic: The Sinitic languages include Mandarin and other Chinese languages (such as Wu, Yue, Jin, Min and Hakka).",Lamkang,Sino-Tibetan
6,Endangered Languages Project - Lamkang - Lamkang Language...,https://www.endangeredlanguages.com/lang/849/samples/8807,"Lamkang. Classification: Sino-Tibetan.Home page of the Lamkang Language Resource website. Provides information about the language and ongoing documentation efforts, as well as a collection of texts.",Lamkang,Sino-Tibetan
7,Lamkang,https://docs.verbix.com/EndangeredLanguages/Lamkang,"Language family: Sino-Tibetan, Tibeto-Burman, Sal, Kuki-Chin-Naga, Kuki-Chin, Northern (SIL classification). Number of speakers: 5,894.A Scheduled Tribe. Christian. Lamkang is spoken in India, South Asia.",Lamkang,Sino-Tibetan
